WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.085 --> 00:00:05.672
Benvolio was struggling with some of the Capulets when he heard Romeo's furious challenge to Tybalt.

2
00:00:05.673 --> 00:00:10.267
He turned around just in time to see Romeo rush after Tybalt with his sword drawn.

3
00:00:10.268 --> 00:00:16.601
News of the fight had spread quickly through Verona, and now others, including Montagues, were arriving in the square.

4
00:00:16.602 --> 00:00:22.437
Benvolio pulled Mercutio's lifeless body out of the way, then headed back to find his friend.

5
00:00:22.438 --> 00:00:30.085
The fight had escalated into a terrible brawl. Capulets and Montagues were yelling and fighting with their fists raised and swords drawn.

6
00:00:30.086 --> 00:00:34.260
Citizens of the city were grabbing at the feuding family members to keep them apart.

7
00:00:34.261 --> 00:00:38.093
People were calling for the prince and his soldiers to come and restore order.

8
00:00:38.094 --> 00:00:44.014
Pushing through the crowd, Benvolio caught a glimpse of Tybalt slashing at Romeo in a far corner of the square.

9
00:00:44.015 --> 00:00:48.845
He rushed toward them. When he arrived, he found his friend standing over Tybalt's body.

10
00:00:48.846 --> 00:00:56.032
Romeo was panting heavily and there was a gash on his arm, but Tybalt lay cold and still on the cobblestones.

11
00:00:56.033 --> 00:00:58.408
"I've killed him," said Romeo.

12
00:00:58.409 --> 00:01:02.397
Just then, they both heard a trumpet blare as soldiers arrived in the square.

13
00:01:02.397 --> 00:01:08.316
"Romeo, you must run!" said Benvolio. "If they find you, the prince will sentence you to death."

14
00:01:08.317 --> 00:01:09.967
But Romeo didn't answer.

15
00:01:09.975 --> 00:01:14.866
Benvolio grabbed his arm and said, "Why are you waiting? Run! Flee!"

16
00:01:14.867 --> 00:01:16.839
Romeo looked into his friend's eyes.

17
00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:23.045
He knew that Benvolio was right and, pausing only to hug his friend goodbye, he disappeared down an alley.

18
00:01:23.046 --> 00:01:28.037
Quickly Benvolio headed back to the main square, where the soldiers had separated the brawlers.

19
00:01:28.038 --> 00:01:33.196
The prince strode into the square and demanded, "Who is responsible for this bloodshed and disgrace?"

20
00:01:33.197 --> 00:01:36.418
"Here's one of them!" shouted a soldier, grabbing Benvolio.

21
00:01:36.419 --> 00:01:41.116
When he stood before the prince, Benvolio stepped away from the soldier and bowed with dignity.

22
00:01:41.117 --> 00:01:51.370
He clearly explained the entire sequence of events: how Tybalt had slain Mercutio, and how Romeo had slain Tybalt in revenge and then fled.

23
00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:55.282
Despite the soldiers holding them back, both families began to shout.

24
00:01:55.283 --> 00:02:00.734
The Capulets wanted Romeo's death; the Montagues pleaded for mercy and for Romeo's life.

25
00:02:00.735 --> 00:02:04.004
The prince raised his hand and the crowd went quiet.

26
00:02:04.005 --> 00:02:10.522
"I will show mercy only this far: Romeo is hereby exiled from Verona," announced the prince.

27
00:02:10.523 --> 00:02:15.046
"But the next time he is found in the city, that hour is his last."

28
00:02:15.047 --> 00:02:18.896
News of the fight and Romeo's exile spread through the city quickly.

29
00:02:18.896 --> 00:02:23.625
When the news reached the Capulet house, Lady Capulet moaned in grief at her nephew's death.

30
00:02:23.626 --> 00:02:28.830
"Oh Tybalt! Tybalt!" she sobbed, and laid her head on her husband's shoulder.

31
00:02:28.831 --> 00:02:33.219
Lord Capulet tried to calm her as the nurse ran upstairs to tell Juliet.

32
00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:40.117
"What terrible news!" said the nurse, coming into Juliet's room. "He's dead. He's been killed!"

33
00:02:40.118 --> 00:02:45.657
"Who? Romeo?" gasped Juliet, still thinking of her wedding earlier that afternoon.

34
00:02:45.658 --> 00:02:50.784
Too upset to tell a long-winded story, the nurse quickly gave Juliet the heavy news.

35
00:02:50.785 --> 00:02:55.137
"No, your cousin Tybalt. And Romeo is banished for killing him!"

36
00:02:55.138 --> 00:02:57.999
Juliet was caught in a turmoil of grief.

37
00:02:57.999 --> 00:03:01.697
At first, she raged against her husband for her cousin's death.

38
00:03:01.698 --> 00:03:09.006
When she learned that Tybalt had wanted to kill Romeo, her tears of sorrow turned into tears of joy because he had escaped.

39
00:03:09.007 --> 00:03:15.363
Then she fell on her bed in a fresh flood of tears when she realized that she might never see her love again.

40
00:03:15.364 --> 00:03:17.664
The nurse didn't know how to comfort her.

41
00:03:17.665 --> 00:03:23.831
At last, she decided to leave Juliet and find Friar Lawrence in the hopes of getting a message to Romeo.

42
00:03:23.832 --> 00:03:31.522
Meanwhile Romeo, after making his way secretly through the streets of Verona, decided to take refuge at Friar Lawrence's monastery.

43
00:03:31.523 --> 00:03:36.879
There the friar bandaged Romeo's arm and told him about the prince's sentence.

44
00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:41.363
"Banishment? This is worse than death!" cried Romeo.

45
00:03:41.364 --> 00:03:47.586
"Heaven is here where Juliet lives! The world outside Verona's walls is torture─it is hell itself!"

46
00:03:47.587 --> 00:03:50.543
His emotions, like Juliet's, were in turmoil.

47
00:03:50.544 --> 00:03:54.435
He pulled his dagger from his belt and held it, looking at his hand.

48
00:03:54.436 --> 00:04:01.445
"This hand is cursed," he said. "It murdered Juliet's cousin and stained our joy with her own family's blood!"

49
00:04:01.446 --> 00:04:04.976
Just then, Juliet's nurse arrived at the monastery and knocked.

50
00:04:04.977 --> 00:04:08.363
The friar answered the door and they spoke briefly.

51
00:04:08.364 --> 00:04:14.522
Without revealing that Romeo was inside, the friar promised to let Romeo know of Juliet's suffering.

52
00:04:14.523 --> 00:04:23.809
When he came back inside, he saw such a desperate look in Romeo's eyes that, for a moment, he was afraid Romeo might use his dagger on himself.

53
00:04:23.809 --> 00:04:28.157
"Will you kill yourself? Put your weapon away!" he said.

54
00:04:28.158 --> 00:04:33.109
"You have every reason to be happy. Tybalt tried to kill you, but you defeated him.

55
00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:38.459
By law, you should be sentenced to death, but the prince softened your sentence to banishment.

56
00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:41.522
And Juliet is alive and calling for you."

57
00:04:41.524 --> 00:04:45.293
At the thought of Juliet, Romeo stood up and put his dagger away.

58
00:04:45.293 --> 00:04:53.340
Friar Lawrence told him to visit his love that night, as they had originally planned, but warned him to escape to nearby Mantua before morning.

59
00:04:53.341 --> 00:04:58.229
"When the time is right, I'll announce your marriage and joyfully reconcile your families.

60
00:04:58.230 --> 00:05:06.872
Then the prince cannot refuse to pardon you, and you can return," he explained. "While you're away, I'll send letters with news."

61
00:05:06.873 --> 00:05:15.340
So that night, when Romeo snuck into the garden where he and Juliet had first vowed their love, he found a ladder hidden just where they had planned.

62
00:05:15.341 --> 00:05:19.497
He climbed up, and Juliet happily welcomed him onto her balcony.

63
00:05:19.498 --> 00:05:25.523
Together, they smiled for the first time since the horrible events in the city square that afternoon.

64
00:05:25.524 --> 00:05:33.332
Downstairs in the front hall, Juliet's parents were just saying goodbye to Paris, the count who wanted to marry Juliet.

65
00:05:33.333 --> 00:05:36.824
"Juliet won't come down tonight," said Lady Capulet.

66
00:05:36.825 --> 00:05:39.497
"She is too distraught over Tybalt's death."

67
00:05:39.498 --> 00:05:42.365
"These are sad times," said Paris.

68
00:05:42.365 --> 00:05:46.254
"My wife will talk to my daughter in the morning," Lord Capulet said.

69
00:05:46.255 --> 00:05:49.500
"I promise you, in three days, the two of you will marry."

70
00:05:49.501 --> 00:05:53.801
"I thank you," said Paris, and they shook hands before saying goodnight.

71
00:05:53.809 --> 00:05:58.713
As dawn approached, the birds began to chirp and the sky grew lighter in the east.

72
00:05:58.714 --> 00:06:01.495
Romeo knew it was time for him to leave.

73
00:06:01.496 --> 00:06:03.873
"Will you go?" said Juliet.

74
00:06:03.873 --> 00:06:08.197
She also knew it was time for Romeo to leave, but she couldn't bear to part.

75
00:06:08.198 --> 00:06:15.673
"The day isn't near. That bird you heard was the nightingale, not the lark. It sings in our garden every night."

76
00:06:15.674 --> 00:06:17.151
Romeo smiled.

77
00:06:17.152 --> 00:06:20.925
"Oh believe me, love," she said. "It was the nightingale."

78
00:06:20.926 --> 00:06:24.817
"It was the lark," said Romeo, "the herald of the morning."

79
00:06:24.817 --> 00:06:27.800
But Romeo too did not want to leave.

80
00:06:27.801 --> 00:06:34.942
"Together, dear Juliet, we'll pretend that the sun doesn't brighten the clouds in the east, but some strange meteor instead.

81
00:06:34.943 --> 00:06:39.232
Let me be captured and put to death, as long as I never have to say goodbye."

82
00:06:39.233 --> 00:06:45.368
At these words, Juliet remembered the danger he faced, and her longing suddenly changed to fear.

83
00:06:45.369 --> 00:06:47.377
She threw her arms around his neck.

84
00:06:47.378 --> 00:06:52.524
"It was the lark! The stars have gone and the sky grows light! Oh, you must go!"

85
00:06:52.525 --> 00:06:56.205
Romeo stepped to the ladder and climbed over the balcony's rail.

86
00:06:56.206 --> 00:07:03.214
"Farewell, farewell. One kiss, and I'll descend," he said, and their lips touched one last time.

87
00:07:03.215 --> 00:07:07.710
Then, without a word, Romeo climbed down and was gone.

